What is React?

React is a JavaScript library for building interactive user interfaces. It follows a component-based architecture, allowing us to create reusable UI components. 
React uses state and props to manage data and re-renders components when their relevant data changes, keeping the UI synchronized with the application state.

Why React?

Let's first see how we create a page using plain HTML.

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
    <title>Learning React</title>

    <body>
        <h1>Hi, My name is Rohan</h1>
    </body>
</html>

Here the UI is static.

name = Rohan

       ↓

Hi, My name is Rohan

Now suppose the name comes from some JavaScript logic and changes every 5 seconds.

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
    <title>Learning React</title>

    <body>
        <div id="root"></div>

        <script>
            let name = "Rohan";

            setInterval(() => {
                name = name === "Rohan" ? "Mike" : "Rohan";
                console.log(name);
            }, 5000);

            const header = document.createElement("h1");

            header.innerHTML = "Hi, My name is " + name;

            const root = document.getElementById("root");

            root.appendChild(header);
        </script>
    </body>
</html>

Here, the value of name changes:

Rohan
  ↓ 5 seconds
Mike
  ↓ 5 seconds
Rohan
  ↓ 5 seconds
Mike

But the UI remains:

Hi, My name is Rohan

Why?
Because changing a JavaScript variable does not automatically update the DOM.

We initially did:

header.innerHTML = "Hi, My name is " + name;

At that point:

name = Rohan

So the browser displays:

Hi, My name is Rohan

Later, when:

name = "Mike";

the variable changes, but the browser doesn't know that it needs to change the <h1>.

Updating the UI manually
With plain JavaScript, we have to explicitly update the DOM whenever the value changes.

<script>
    let name = "Rohan";

    const header = document.createElement("h1");
    const root = document.getElementById("root");

    setInterval(() => {
        name = name === "Rohan" ? "Mike" : "Rohan";

        header.innerHTML = "Hi, My name is " + name;

        root.appendChild(header);
    }, 5000);
</script>

Now:

name changes
     ↓
manually update DOM
     ↓
UI changes

So we have to write the code responsible for keeping the UI synchronized with our data.

The problem
Imagine a large application with:

hundreds of UI elements
many changing values
user interactions
API responses
forms
conditional UI
nested components
Manually finding and updating every affected DOM element becomes difficult to maintain.

We would have to continuously think:

"Which value changed?"
        ↓
"Which DOM element depends on it?"
        ↓
"How should I update that element?"
        ↓
"Are there other elements that depend on the same value?"

This is where React helps.

What React gives us
React allows us to describe the UI based on the application's state.

Conceptually:

State
  ↓
UI

When state changes:

State changes
     ↓
React detects the change
     ↓
React determines what UI needs to change
     ↓
React updates the DOM

So instead of manually doing:

header.innerHTML = ...

every time our data changes, we tell React:

"This UI should look like this based on the current state."

React takes care of updating the UI.
